[1mNAME[0m
       git-diff - Show changes between commits, commit and working tree, etc

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] [<commit>] [--] [<path>...]
       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] --cached [--merge-base] [<commit>] [--] [<path>...]
       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] [--merge-base] <commit> [<commit>...] <commit> [--] [<path>...]
       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] <commit>...<commit> [--] [<path>...]
       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] <blob> <blob>
       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] --no-index [--] <path> <path>

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       Show changes between the working tree and the index or a tree, changes between the index and a tree, changes between two trees, changes resulting from a merge, changes between two blob objects, or changes between
       two files on disk.

       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] [--] [<path>...]
           This form is to view the changes you made relative to the index (staging area for the next commit). In other words, the differences are what you [4mcould[24m tell Git to further add to the index but you still haven’t.
           You can stage these changes by using [1mgit-add[22m(1).

       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] --no-index [--] <path> <path>
           This form is to compare the given two paths on the filesystem. You can omit the [1m--no-index [22moption when running the command in a working tree controlled by Git and at least one of the paths points outside the
           working tree, or when running the command outside a working tree controlled by Git. This form implies [1m--exit-code[22m.

       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] --cached [--merge-base] [<commit>] [--] [<path>...]
           This form is to view the changes you staged for the next commit relative to the named <commit>. Typically you would want comparison with the latest commit, so if you do not give <commit>, it defaults to HEAD. If
           HEAD does not exist (e.g. unborn branches) and <commit> is not given, it shows all staged changes. --staged is a synonym of --cached.

           If --merge-base is given, instead of using <commit>, use the merge base of <commit> and HEAD.  [1mgit diff --cached --merge-base A [22mis equivalent to [1mgit diff --cached $(git merge-base A HEAD)[22m.

       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] [--merge-base] <commit> [--] [<path>...]
           This form is to view the changes you have in your working tree relative to the named <commit>. You can use HEAD to compare it with the latest commit, or a branch name to compare with the tip of a different
           branch.

           If --merge-base is given, instead of using <commit>, use the merge base of <commit> and HEAD.  [1mgit diff --merge-base A [22mis equivalent to [1mgit diff $(git merge-base A HEAD)[22m.

       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] [--merge-base] <commit> <commit> [--] [<path>...]
           This is to view the changes between two arbitrary <commit>.

           If --merge-base is given, use the merge base of the two commits for the "before" side.  [1mgit diff --merge-base A B [22mis equivalent to [1mgit diff $(git merge-base A B) B[22m.

       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] <commit> <commit>... <commit> [--] [<path>...]
           This form is to view the results of a merge commit. The first listed <commit> must be the merge itself; the remaining two or more commits should be its parents. Convenient ways to produce the desired set of
           revisions are to use the suffixes [1m^@ [22mand [1m^![22m. If A is a merge commit, then [1mgit diff A A^@[22m, [1mgit diff A^!  [22mand [1mgit show A [22mall give the same combined diff.

       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] <commit>..<commit> [--] [<path>...]
           This is synonymous to the earlier form (without the [1m..[22m) for viewing the changes between two arbitrary <commit>. If <commit> on one side is omitted, it will have the same effect as using HEAD instead.

       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] <commit>...<commit> [--] [<path>...]
           This form is to view the changes on the branch containing and up to the second <commit>, starting at a common ancestor of both <commit>.  [1mgit diff A...B [22mis equivalent to [1mgit diff $(git merge-base A B) B[22m. You can
           omit any one of <commit>, which has the same effect as using HEAD instead.

       Just in case you are doing something exotic, it should be noted that all of the <commit> in the above description, except in the [1m--merge-base [22mcase and in the last two forms that use [1m.. [22mnotations, can be any <tree>.
       A tree of interest is the one pointed to by the special ref [1mAUTO_MERGE[22m, which is written by the [4mort[24m merge strategy upon hitting merge conflicts (see [1mgit-merge[22m(1)). Comparing the working tree with [1mAUTO_MERGE [22mshows
       changes you’ve made so far to resolve textual conflicts (see the examples below).

       For a more complete list of ways to spell <commit>, see "SPECIFYING REVISIONS" section in [1mgitrevisions[22m(7). However, "diff" is about comparing two [4mendpoints[24m, not ranges, and the range notations ([1m<commit>..<commit>[0m
       and [1m<commit>...<commit>[22m) do not mean a range as defined in the "SPECIFYING RANGES" section in [1mgitrevisions[22m(7).

       [4mgit[24m [4mdiff[24m [<options>] <blob> <blob>
           This form is to view the differences between the raw contents of two blob objects.

